UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 13, Lesson 1: Equivalent fractions', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Equivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal shaded amounts.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nName two equivalents of 1/3 using a model or rule.\n\nFEEDBACK\n2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third.\n\nWATCH FOR\nChanging only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind three equivalents each for six unit fractions. Draw models for four pairs and write the multiply rule you used.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Equivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal shaded amounts.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-PF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find three equivalents each for six unit fractions. Draw models for four pairs and write the multiply rule you used. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: 2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third. Reasoning: I used equivalent by attending to fraction rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence. Correction: 2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third. Why: the correction restores accurate use of equivalent.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses equivalent with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with equivalent, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third. This matches the lesson skill for equivalent.","options":["Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third."],"question":"Name two equivalents of 1/3 using a model or rule. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence.","Equivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal shaded amounts.","2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third.","Careful use of fraction with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising equivalent?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named fraction, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the fraction used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising equivalent. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value.","Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about equivalent?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third.","Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Equivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal… — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Average the two answers without checking either.","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed.","Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method.","Retype the worked model numbers as the answer."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of equivalent?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong.","Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits.","Just add more digits until it looks longer.","Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for equivalent."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses equivalent with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: 2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third. Reasoning: I used equivalent by attending to fraction rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with equivalent, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence. Correction: 2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third. Why: the correction restores accurate use of equivalent.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w13-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise equivalent.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use equivalent with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify fraction and explain why it matters.","I can practise same value independently on a new prompt.","I can notice model needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Equivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal shaded amounts. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Equivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal shaded amounts."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equivalent?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. A strip halved once matches a strip split into four with two shaded. Multiply numerator and denominator by the same number.\n\nTRY FIRST\nName two equivalents of 1/3 using a model or rule.\n\nFEEDBACK\n2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third.","explain":"Equivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal shaded amounts.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Equivalent fraction models.","caption":"Multiply num and den by same number.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">1/2</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">half strip</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">2/4</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">two of four</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. A strip halved once matches a strip split into four with two shaded. Multiply numerator and denominator Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. A strip halved once matches a strip split into four with two shaded. Multiply numerator and denominator Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no working.","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equivalent?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. A strip halved once matches a strip split into four with two shaded. Multiply numerator and denominator by the same number. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Equivalence rule vs broken pairs.","caption":"Three equivalents each.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rule</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">×2/×2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">change den only</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equivalent?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChanging only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence. Here is a more productive model: 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. A strip halved once matches a strip split into four with two shaded. Multiply numerator and denominator by the same number.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n2/6 and 3/9 (or other correct pairs); same value as one-third.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Changing only the denominator without the numerator breaks equivalence. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about equivalent?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEquivalent fractions name the same value with different denominators; area or length models show equal shaded amounts. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 13, Lesson 1: Equivalent fractions","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Fractions and decimals","week":13,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. A strip halved once matches a strip split into four with two shaded. Multiply numerator and denominator by the same number.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Equivalent fraction models.","caption":"Multiply num and den by same number.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">1/2</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">half strip</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">2/4</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">two of four</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Equivalence rule vs broken pairs.","caption":"Three equivalents each.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stro